  "image_name": "1bd49ea27e2450da88660c6bbc0c31ae.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how non-coding RNAs may contribute to ischaemic heart disease. It first places the disease in the heart and shows a simplified tissue environment containing cardiomyocytes, extracellular matrix, fibroblasts, macrophages, and blood vessels. A text box highlights the main pathological features associated with this setting: inflammation, extracellular matrix remodelling and stiffening, vascular dysfunction, cardiac fibrosis, harmful stress on cardiomyocytes, and extracellular release of ncRNAs.\n\nThe lower schematics focus on gene regulation by long non-coding RNAs and related ncRNAs. One mechanism shows lncRNA acting in the nucleus to influence chromatin remodelling around a target gene, thereby altering transcription. Another shows lncRNA affecting RNA polymerase II regulation and transcript production. A separate pathway suggests post-transcriptional control, where lncRNA and circRNA interact with a miRNA-like regulatory complex to influence a target mRNA and promote its degradation or repression.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that ischaemic heart disease involves both structural tissue changes and complex RNA-based regulatory networks, with non-coding RNAs shaping gene expression at chromatin, transcriptional, and mRNA stability levels.",
